issues.body:
### What do you want to change?

Let a host admit a submission inside its own `harness.commit`, after checks of its own. Either of these would do:

1. A public way to call `admitSubmission()` (`packages/durable/src/harness/submissions.ts`). Today only `Conversation.submit()` and compaction call it, and `src/index.ts` doesn't export it. It also takes `now`, `queueModes` and a span, which a host can't supply, so a method that takes the `Tx` and a `SubmissionDraft` and fills those in from the Harness would fit better than the bare function.
2. Or `whenIdle: "reject"` on input drafts. The input is admitted only while a run is going, and an idle conversation rejects it with a typed error, as `whenBusy: "reject"` does with `ConversationBusy`.

We'd prefer the first, because it also covers a host whose check reads its own documents.

### Why?

Our host lets a person message an agent while it works. The message must join the current run as a steer and must never start a run of its own, because nothing on our side follows a run we didn't start.

`Conversation.submit({ whenBusy: "steer" })` admits in a commit of its own. If the run ends between our check and that commit, `submit` places the input and starts a new run. `whenBusy: "reject"` refuses when busy, and we need to refuse when idle.

So we admit by hand, in one commit, from what the package exports:

```ts
await harness.commit(async (tx) => {
	// our own checks, on our own documents, then:
	if ((await tx.doc(LiveDoc, id)).run === undefined) throw new Refusal("The agent isn't working now.");
	if ((await tx.submissionByRequest(id, requestId)) !== undefined) return;
	const { id: message } = await tx.createSubmission({ conversationId: id, requestId, type: "input", status: "queued" });
	(await tx.doc(InboxDoc, id)).items.push({ id: message, mode: "steer", content: text });
}, context);
```

`Tx.createSubmission`'s doc allows this ("unless the caller implements admission itself"), but our copy of admission drifts from Pi's. On main, `admitSubmission()` also stores a trace carrier on the submission and links a steer to the running trace, and our copy does neither. The next change to `InboxItem` or to admission could break our steer with nothing to tell us.

### How? (optional)

For the first option, something like `conversation.admit(tx, draft)`, returning the same `{ id, status }` as the internal `Admission`, with the same request-ID dedup, queueing and boundary rules as `submit()`.

### What happened?

The openai-completions stream handler re-parses the **entire accumulated tool-call argument JSON on every delta**, making the cost of streaming one tool call quadratic in its total size. In an embedded runtime (a single-threaded daemon hosting many agent sessions in one process), a model that streams a degenerate/repetitive tool-call argument payload into the megabytes starves the event loop permanently: the parse backlog grows faster than it drains, timers stop firing, and every request in the process hangs. We hit exactly this in production.

This is the same failure family as #8648 (the `reasoning_details` accumulator in this same loop, parse+push+stringify per chunk), which was fixed by appending to an in-memory array and stringifying once. The `partialArgs` accumulator looks like the remaining sibling.

### Relevant code

`packages/ai/src/api/openai-completions.ts`, per `tool_calls` delta (~line 455 in the 0.85.1 build):

```js
block.partialArgs = (block.partialArgs ?? "") + toolCall.function.arguments;
block.arguments = parseStreamingJson(block.partialArgs);
```

`parseStreamingJson` (`packages/ai/src/utils/json-parse.ts`) makes up to four full passes over the accumulated string per delta: a `JSON.parse` attempt, `repairJson` (a char-by-char string rebuild), a re-parse, then `partial-json` over the whole string — and the same pair again over the repaired copy. Each pass allocates an O(n) string, so per-delta cost is O(n) and a whole stream is O(n²).

The `pi-messages` event converter (`createEventConverter`) has a second instance of the same pattern: it accumulates `toolJson` per `contentIndex` and calls `parseStreamingJson(json)` on every `toolcall_delta`.

Text and thinking deltas are fine (O(1) appends); only tool-call arguments are affected.

### Steps to reproduce

Call the shipped `parseStreamingJson` exactly the way the stream loop does — full reparse of the accumulated args per 8 KB delta:

```js
import { parseStreamingJson } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/dist/utils/json-parse.js";

const chunk = "a".repeat(8 * 1024);
let acc = "";
const t0 = performance.now();
while (acc.length < 1024 * 1024) {
  acc += chunk;
  parseStreamingJson(acc);
}
console.log(`1 MB of streamed args: ${(performance.now() - t0).toFixed(0)} ms of parsing`);
```

Measured here (Node 24.13.1): 64 KB → 16 ms cumulative; 256 KB → 110 ms; **1 MB → ~1,400 ms, with the final chunk alone at ~50 ms**. Extrapolating, once accumulated args reach a few MB the per-delta cost exceeds the provider's chunk arrival rate and the loop saturates: ~100% CPU, mostly GC under the string churn, no observable progress.

### Expected behavior

Appending a delta should cost O(delta). The growing `partialArgs` only needs parsing when something reads `block.arguments` — `toolcall_end` (or a throttled/incremental parse if live args are wanted mid-stream), not every delta.

### Version

`@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` 0.85.1 (`@earendil-works/pi-ai` 0.85.1). Reproduced against the standalone `@earendil-works/pi-ai` package and observed in the copy bundled inside `pi-coding-agent`.

### Environment

Embedded `AgentSession` runtime (many sessions in one Node process); provider was an OpenAI-compatible endpoint streaming `tool_calls` deltas. Related: #8648.

> AI disclosure: an AI assistant drafted this report. I checked the cited lines and the repro output against 1.3.0 before posting.

### What happened?

Claude requests fail with `400 … messages.33: role 'system' must precede an 'assistant' message or end the array`. The cause is a mid-conversation `system` message that ends up directly before a `user` message.

In `convertMessages`, held system messages are flushed when an assistant message is reached ([anthropic-messages.ts L1432](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/c268154ca1171c2f9eefe75d3d7372a68aada2d5/packages/ai/src/api/anthropic-messages.ts#L1432)). The function checks afterwards whether that assistant has any blocks, and skips it if it has none ([L1486](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/c268154ca1171c2f9eefe75d3d7372a68aada2d5/packages/ai/src/api/anthropic-messages.ts#L1486)). In the published 1.3.0 build these are `dist/api/anthropic-messages.js` L1124 and L1180. The ordering dates back to #9548.

An assistant converts to zero blocks when it comes from another model and has nothing that can be carried over. In my case these were `openai-codex` replies containing only encrypted reasoning, which `transformMessages` drops ([transform-messages.ts L132](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/c268154ca1171c2f9eefe75d3d7372a68aada2d5/packages/ai/src/api/transform-messages.ts#L132)). Once that happens, every Claude request on the session fails.

When a `before_agent_start` handler returns `systemPrompt`, the forced-prompt projection merges all system messages into the leading one, which hides the bug. Before 1.2.0 it showed up only on `sendMessage({ triggerTurn: true })` turns (#5581).

Suggested fix: call `flushPendingSystemMessages()` only after the `blocks.length === 0` check.

### Steps to reproduce

Run offline with pi-ai only (no extensions). `onPayload` captures the payload without sending it:

```js
import { stream } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/api/anthropic-messages";
import { ANTHROPIC_MODELS } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/providers/anthropic.models";

const model = ANTHROPIC_MODELS["claude-opus-5-5"];

const t = 1;
const usage = { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0, totalTokens: 0,
  cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0, total: 0 } };
const messages = [
  { role: "system", content: "", sections: { preamble: "You are helpful." }, timestamp: t },
  { role: "user", content: [{ type: "text", text: "hi" }], timestamp: t },
  { role: "system", content: "", sections: { extra: "<extra>late section</extra>" }, timestamp: t },
  // Another provider's turn whose only block is encrypted reasoning: dropped for Claude.
  { role: "assistant", content: [{ type: "thinking", thinking: "", thinkingSignature: "{\"type\":\"reasoning\"}" }],
    api: "openai-codex-responses", provider: "openai-codex", model: "gpt-x", stopReason: "stop", usage, timestamp: t },
  { role: "user", content: [{ type: "text", text: "again" }], timestamp: t },
];

let payload;
for await (const _ of stream(model, { messages }, {
  apiKey: "sk-ant-dummy",
  onPayload: (p) => { payload = p; throw new Error("captured"); },
})) {}
console.log(payload.messages.map((m) => m.role + (m.output_config ? "(directive)" : "")).join(" -> "));
```

Output: `user -> system -> user -> system(directive)`

### Expected behavior

The system update is held until the next assistant message that is actually emitted, or until the end of the array: `user -> user -> system -> system(directive)`.

### Version

pi-ai / pi-coding-agent 1.3.0. The same ordering is still on `main` at c268154.

# Issue for badlogic/pi-mono

**Title:** commandExists('fd') passes for fdclone, which hijacks terminal via /dev/tty

**File with:**
```bash
gh auth login
gh issue create --repo badlogic/pi-mono --title "commandExists('fd') passes for fdclone, which hijacks terminal via /dev/tty" --body-file fdclone-issue-body.md
```

---

## Problem

`commandExists()` in `tools-manager.js` (line 66-74) only checks if a binary can be spawned, not if it's the correct tool:

```js
function commandExists(cmd) {
    try {
        const result = spawnSync(cmd, ["--version"], { stdio: "pipe" });
        return result.error === undefined || result.error === null;
    } catch {
        return false;
    }
}
```

On systems where the `fdclone` package is installed (provides `/usr/bin/fd`), this check passes because fdclone spawns successfully (result.error is null) — even though it exits with code 2 and outputs "No such device or address" instead of a version string.

## Impact

When the TUI's autocomplete triggers `walkDirectoryWithFd()`, it spawns `fd` with sharkdp/fd arguments. fdclone opens `/dev/tty` directly (bypassing piped stdio), takes full control of the terminal, and displays its file manager UI. The user sees a Japanese file manager instead of the coding agent.

Confirmed via strace:
```
openat(AT_FDCWD, "/dev/tty", O_RDWR) = 3
```

This means even `stdio: ["pipe", "pipe", "pipe"]` in `walkDirectoryWithFd` cannot prevent fdclone from taking over.

## Suggested fix

Validate the output in `commandExists`, e.g.:

```js
function commandExists(cmd) {
    try {
        const result = spawnSync(cmd, ["--version"], { stdio: "pipe" });
        if (result.error !== undefined && result.error !== null) return false;
        if (result.status !== 0) return false;
        return true;
    } catch {
        return false;
    }
}
```

Checking `result.status === 0` would reject fdclone (exits with code 2) while accepting sharkdp/fd (exits with code 0).

## Environment

- Ubuntu with `fdclone` package installed (`/usr/bin/fd`)
- pi-coding-agent v0.58.x
- Discovered while using clawx (`@halfagiraf/clawx`), which wraps pi-coding-agent's InteractiveMode

prs.body:
fixes #10740

`arrayType` checked `items` before `prefixItems`, so 2020-12 tuples rendered as plain arrays: `{ prefixItems: [string, number], items: false }` became `Array<never>` instead of `[string, number]`. This checks `prefixItems` first and renders `items` as a rest element when it is a schema, e.g. `[string, number, ...Array<boolean>]`. Plain arrays and older `items: [A, B]` tuples are unchanged. Added a regression test.

@badlogic I'm contributing as part of CS146S at Stanford. I used Claude Code to help trace the cause and draft the fix, and I reproduced the bug and reviewed the change myself.

issues.body:
## Summary

`enforceMessageActionConversationReadGate` (the host chokepoint before any read-capable channel-plugin callback runs) can structurally never pass for a session that has **no current conversation at all** — e.g. an isolated/standalone scheduled job (cron, subagent announce) that is not bound to any room. This is a different root cause from the "wrong target resolution within a live conversation" issues already tracked for this same gate (#115367, #140978, #150193): those are about trust/target-matching *within* an active conversation; this is about sessions with no conversation context to match against in the first place.

## Environment

- OpenClaw 2026.9.5 (ec9c1a1)
- Node v24.21.0, Linux x64

## Source evidence

`dist/message-action-normalization-C7ZjTUQv.mjs:894-912`:

```js
function resolveMessageActionConversationReadGate(params) {
	if (params.actionPolicy.kind === "none" || params.origin === "direct-operator") return true;
	if (params.enforcement.kind === "provider-owned") {
		if (params.enforcement.fenced && params.enforcement.pluginTrust === "external" && !params.scheduledAccess && !params.assertDashboardReadCurrent && (!hasMatchingCurrentProviderContext(params.ctx) || !hasMatchingCurrentAccountContext(params.ctx) || !hasCurrentConversationTarget(params.ctx))) throw new Error(`Delegated ${params.ctx.channel}:${params.ctx.action} requires current provider and account context.`);
		return true;
	}
	return params.enforcement.pluginTrust === "bundled" && params.actionPolicy.targetlessCache === "bundled-current-context" && hasMatchingCurrentProviderContext(params.ctx) && hasMatchingCurrentAccountContext(params.ctx) && hasCurrentConversationTarget(params.ctx) || resolveExactCurrentConversationMatch({ ... });
}
function enforceMessageActionConversationReadMatch(params, matches) {
	if (!matches) throw new Error(`Delegated ${params.ctx.channel}:${params.ctx.action} requires the exact current conversation and account for this plugin.`);
	...
}
```

Every non-`direct-operator`, non-`none`-policy branch requires `hasCurrentConversationTarget(params.ctx)` (or an equivalent exact-current-conversation match) to be true. A session created with no bound conversation (isolated cron/subagent-announce session invoking `message` with a `read`-family action against some other room) has nothing for `hasCurrentConversationTarget` to match — the check is unsatisfiable by construction, not by misconfiguration.

## Repro

1. Create a scheduled job whose session has no paired room/channel (an "isolated" session).
2. From inside that job's turn, call `message(action="read", ...)` targeting some room.
3. The gate throws `Delegated <channel>:read requires the exact current conversation and account for this plugin.` every time, regardless of any config.

Observed first-hand 2026-09-10 on a Matrix-channel deployment: an isolated cron job's `message read` call failed with this exact error text, while the job's own completion status still reported success (the job caught the thrown error and returned it as its own reply text, so the run was marked delivered/ok even though the read failed) — the plumbing that maps this exception to job status is a separate, related concern worth flagging but not verified at the source level here.

Also observed: `openclaw message read` via the CLI succeeds for the same target from the same host, which does not appear to route through this gate — worth confirming whether that is an intentional exemption or a second, undocumented bypass path.

## Expected vs actual

- **Expected:** either isolated/unbound sessions get an explicit, documented "reads are not permitted from a session with no conversation context" behavior, or they're given a way to pass a minimal conversation context for a legitimate delegated read.
- **Actual:** the gate throws the same generic "requires the exact current conversation" error a misconfigured trust boundary would throw, with no distinction for "this session type structurally cannot satisfy this check."

## Impact

Medium — isolated cron/scheduled jobs that need to read message history (a common pattern for periodic digest/summary jobs) cannot do so via the `message` tool at all; the failure mode is a generic error string indistinguishable from a plugin-trust misconfiguration, which cost real debugging time.

## Suggested fix

1. Detect the "no current conversation, ever" case distinctly from "current conversation doesn't match" and surface a clearer error (e.g. "this session has no bound conversation; delegated reads require a paired room").
2. Document whether isolated/scheduled sessions are expected to use a different code path (e.g. the CLI's) for reads.

**Suggested labels**: `channel: feishu`, `enhancement`

## Problem

Since streaming-card support landed, final replies containing markdown code fences are sent as `msg_type: "interactive"` cards, because `shouldUseCard()` treats fences (and md tables) as card triggers:

```js
function shouldUseCard(text) {
    return /```[\s\S]*?```/.test(text) || /\|.+\|[\r\n]+\|[-:| ]+\|/.test(text);
}
```

But the Feishu client renders the **language label + copy button** on code blocks *only* when the message is a **post** whose entire content is a **single `md` element**:

```json
{"zh_cn": {"content": [[{"tag": "md", "text": "<whole markdown, fences as-is>"}]]}}
```

Interactive cards (v1/v2, streaming_mode, summary — any combination), multi-element posts, and per-fence split posts all fall back to a bare markdown renderer **without the copy button**, regardless of the language tag in the fence. Verified with an A~K send-shape matrix on the Feishu macOS/iOS clients; the Hermes project independently reproduced the same matrix.

## Impact

Code-heavy answers — the most common output of coding agents — lose the copy affordance for every Feishu channel user. `renderMode: "raw"` avoids cards but is a blunt switch: it also disables streaming previews, card table rendering and control buttons for *all* messages.

## Requested behavior

Per-content routing at final-reply time: if the final text contains complete ```` ``` ```` fences, send it as a single-md-element **post** (chunk oversized content outside the fences, each chunk self-contained); keep streaming previews, table cards and control cards as-is.

Concretely: extend `renderMode` with a `"post-on-code"` value (or make `auto` fence-aware in this direction).

## Prior art

`lark-channel-bridge` (github.com/zarazhangrui/lark-coding-agent-bridge, ~2.5k★) already ships this exact pattern: streaming via interactive card, final answer as a single-element post.

## Reference patch

Minimal local patch against v2026.9.4 included below (`<details>` block — compiled-dist patch, 2 files / 4 changed lines): it only excludes fence-containing finals from the card decision points; the existing `buildFeishuPostMessageContent` already produces the exact single-md-element shape and the existing chunker already cuts outside fences. Happy to open a PR.

## Verified after patching (live, Feishu client)

- single fenced final → post with `bash` label + copy button ✔
- two fenced blocks in one final → one post, both blocks copyable ✔
- 4.3k-char fenced final → 3 chunks, every fence closed ✔
- plain final / table final → card, unchanged (no regression) ✔


<details>
<summary>Reference patch applied to the compiled dist of @openclaw/feishu v2026.9.4 (2 files, 4 changed lines)</summary>

````diff
--- a/node_modules/@openclaw/feishu/dist/channel-HDnQAH4Z.js (before patch)
+++ b/node_modules/@openclaw/feishu/dist/channel-HDnQAH4Z.js (after patch)
@@ -863,7 +863,7 @@
 		cfg,
 		accountId
 	}).config?.renderMode ?? "auto";
-	const useCard = (renderMode === "card" || renderMode === "auto" && shouldUseCard(text)) && withinCardTableLimit(text);
+	const useCard = (renderMode === "card" || renderMode === "auto" && shouldUseCard(text)) && withinCardTableLimit(text) && !/```[\s\S]*?```/.test(text);
 	const tableMode = resolveMarkdownTableMode({
 		cfg,
 		channel: "feishu"
````

````diff
--- a/node_modules/@openclaw/feishu/dist/monitor.account-CZ7EtqYS.js (before patch)
+++ b/node_modules/@openclaw/feishu/dist/monitor.account-CZ7EtqYS.js (after patch)
@@ -2432,11 +2432,11 @@
 				}));
 				const finalTextExceedsStreamingLimit = info?.kind === "final" && hasText && text.length > textChunkLimit;
 				const cardRenderingRequested = renderMode === "card" || info?.kind === "block" && coreBlockStreamingEnabled && renderMode !== "raw" || renderMode === "auto" && shouldUseCard(text);
-				const useStaticCard = hasText && cardRenderingRequested && withinCardTableLimit(text);
-				const useStreamingCard = hasText && streamingEnabled && !finalTextExceedsStreamingLimit && (info?.kind === "final" || cardRenderingRequested);
+				const useStaticCard = hasText && cardRenderingRequested && withinCardTableLimit(text) && !(info?.kind === "final" && hasText && /```[\s\S]*?```/.test(text));
+				const useStreamingCard = hasText && streamingEnabled && !finalTextExceedsStreamingLimit && !(info?.kind === "final" && hasText && /```[\s\S]*?```/.test(text)) && (info?.kind === "final" || cardRenderingRequested);
 				const skipTextForDuplicateFinal = !hasIndependentPresentation && info?.kind === "final" && hasText && deliveredFinalTexts.has(text);
 				const shouldDeliverText = hasText && (!hasVoiceMedia || hasPresentationFallback) && !skipTextForDuplicateFinal;
-				const shouldDiscardStreamingPreview = info?.kind === "final" && !(hasIndependentPresentation && payload.isError === true && hasStreamingFinalText) && (hasIndependentPresentation || finalTextExceedsStreamingLimit || hasMedia && (hasVoiceMedia && !shouldDeliverText && !ttsTextAlreadyVisible || skipTextForDuplicateFinal));
+				const shouldDiscardStreamingPreview = info?.kind === "final" && !(hasIndependentPresentation && payload.isError === true && hasStreamingFinalText) && (hasIndependentPresentation || finalTextExceedsStreamingLimit || info?.kind === "final" && hasText && /```[\s\S]*?```/.test(text) || hasMedia && (hasVoiceMedia && !shouldDeliverText && !ttsTextAlreadyVisible || skipTextForDuplicateFinal));
 				const priorClosedStreamingSettlement = info?.kind === "final" && hasText && skipTextForDuplicateFinal ? claimClosedStreamingResult(void 0, text) : void 0;
 				if (!shouldDeliverText && !hasMedia && !presentationCard) {
 					if (priorClosedStreamingSettlement?.error !== void 0) throw toStringifiedError(priorClosedStreamingSettlement.error);
````

</details>

### Bug type

Crash (process/app exits or hangs)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

With `agents.defaults.maxConcurrent=1`, a parent awaiting the system-expert `openclaw` tool can hold the only `main` slot while the expert's embedded inference queues for that same slot; the expert's separate outer queue does not prevent this circular wait.

### Steps to reproduce

Deterministic, provider-free source reproduction (do not run the expert against a live installation to reproduce):

1. Use an isolated dependency-complete checkout of OpenClaw `2026.9.4`, commit `3a9d69db306cd7f081e06254cb89c4bcc14a7107`.
2. Save/apply the **focused regression patch** below. Save the two supplied harness files at the checkout root. The fixture has a preverified synthetic conversation and mock model dispatch; it uses the real gateway handler, engine/router, agent-turn callsite, embedded runner admission, command queues, and admission closure. No provider request, node approval, or other operation is performed.
3. Run serially:
   ```sh
   pnpm exec vitest run --config isolated-deadlock.vitest.config.ts src/gateway/server-methods/system-agent-nested-inference.integration.test.ts
   ```
4. On original source, three controls pass and the nested-liveness assertion fails: `main` has one active parent and one queued expert, with zero model dispatches. The fixture cleans up its synthetic work even on failure.
5. Apply the separate **experimental scheduling patch** below; the same four cases pass without increasing main concurrency. The broader original targeted run passed 122 tests across 11 files.

The actual captured runs used Node directly to launch Vitest in an environment scrubbed of provider credentials, with isolated state/config, one worker, and a resource guard. A new clean checkout should install its own dependencies with the repository's frozen-lockfile workflow. Normal dependency-complete build/CI validation is still pending.

### Expected behavior

The expert should complete an allowed synchronous delegated turn while its parent waits, preserving existing authorization and serialization. Controls show standalone inference works when `main` is free, and distinct embedded admission permits the nested case to complete at one slot per lane.

### Actual behavior

The expert never reaches model dispatch until the parent releases `main`. Its unique conversation/session identity and enclosing `system-agent` lane do not remove the global `main` dependency. Generic stalled-session recovery can eventually abort the parent; that does not make the synchronous call succeed.

### OpenClaw version

2026.9.4 — `3a9d69db306cd7f081e06254cb89c4bcc14a7107`.

On 2026-09-13, a read of current `main`'s `src/system-agent/agent-turn.ts` returned the same unpatched blob `e7243ec0b8577df13db85f164c4328222b38c91c`. Current-main execution has not been tested.

### Operating system

Linux, Debian 12 container; Node.js v24.19.0.

### Install method

Official Docker runtime; reproduction and candidate validation in an isolated matching source checkout, not the installed runtime.

### Model

Synthetic model/runtime fixtures for the deterministic reproduction; no real model generation. Effective model identity for the original incident: `NOT_ENOUGH_INFO` in the retained sanitized evidence.

### Provider / routing chain

Reproduction: real `openclaw.chat` handler → outer command/keyed queue → `SystemAgentChatEngine` / router → `runSystemAgentTurnWithDeps` → real embedded orchestrator / session and global lane admission → synthetic dispatch.

Configuration/auth/plugin-owner proofs and transcript persistence are fixtures. This is not a real-provider, native Codex transport, full gateway startup/shutdown, or external-operation test.

### Logs

Deterministic original-source observation:

```json
{"observation":"waiting","main":{"activeCount":1,"queuedCount":1,"maxConcurrent":1,"blockedBy":"lane"},"inference":{"activeCount":0,"queuedCount":0,"maxConcurrent":1}}
```

Sanitized supporting incident evidence on the same version:

```text
stalled session: reason=blocked_tool_call activeTool=openclaw
parent aborted after 902298 ms
immediately afterward: lane=main waitedMs=883104 queueAhead=0 activeAhead=1 activeNow=0 queueBehind=0
```

The queue log lacks a per-expert task identifier; its timing supports but does not by itself prove task attribution. The isolated source reproduction establishes the circular dependency. The node-approval outcome was not verified and was not retried.

### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

Source path:

- [`system-agent-execution.ts`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/3a9d69db306cd7f081e06254cb89c4bcc14a7107/src/gateway/server-methods/system-agent-execution.ts): distinct outer lane plus keyed serialization.
- [`agent-turn.ts`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/3a9d69db306cd7f081e06254cb89c4bcc14a7107/src/system-agent/agent-turn.ts): shared runner parameters omit `lane`; configured planner parameters in [`assistant.ts`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/3a9d69db306cd7f081e06254cb89c4bcc14a7107/src/system-agent/assistant.ts) have the same default-main edge.
- [`lane-controller.ts`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/3a9d69db306cd7f081e06254cb89c4bcc14a7107/src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run/lane-controller.ts): embedded session/global admission.

```text
parent holds main → awaits expert gateway task → expert awaits main → parent
```

The complete focused four-case regression and two-line scheduling experiment are included below. These are the historical red/green fixtures, not a claim that all broader follow-up cases are included in this issue.

### Impact and severity

- Affected: the tested synchronous embedded expert path when its parent occupies the only `main` slot.
- Severity: blocks the delegated workflow until an external cancellation/recovery breaks the wait.
- Frequency: deterministic under the isolated queue topology; one reported live workflow supported by sanitized diagnostics. Broader incidence is `NOT_ENOUGH_INFO`.
- Consequence: no expert reply/verified requested outcome before recovery. No data loss or mutation-authority bypass demonstrated.

### Additional information

Related but distinct: #128851 was closed after generic blocked-tool recovery was implemented. This report concerns the circular admission dependency that makes the expert call stall in the first place; it does **not** claim recovery never aborts.

**Experimental fix only, not a deployment recommendation:** a distinct embedded inference lane fixes the measured circular wait. Reusing the enclosing `system-agent` lane instead merely moves the wait cycle. A maintainable implementation also needs static lane/diagnostic registration and capacity-group ineligibility review.

Additional isolated follow-up reached **247 passing tests across 18 files**, including real-delegate cancellation and direct CLI admission characterizations. Some passing cases deliberately assert retained defects. That work confirms limits rather than broadening this issue's fix claim:

- CLI admission uses a separate backend/session key; a generic serialized backend can still collide regardless of `lane`. Do not disable backend serialization or append arbitrary queue suffixes.
- The turn timeout starts after admission, not during queue waiting. A fake-clock test remained queued after 150,001 ms despite a 120,000 ms turn timeout.
- An experimental private-signal follow-up prevents canceled queued embedded work from later dispatching, but late-directive/fresh-session/wizard and real-provider cancellation remain incomplete.
- Full transitive typecheck was stopped by the unchanged memory guard; full build, live providers, and native transport were not validated. No candidate deployed, live concurrency changed, or node approval retried.

No last-known-good version has been established. Separate Codex connection/policy-handoff failures are not proven to share this cause. The public submission is intentionally scoped to the embedded nested-main circular wait.

