## Problem

WhatsApp users commonly send several images in quick succession and intend them as one request: for example, multiple screenshots followed by “compare these”, or several photos of the same object from different angles.

Current main deliberately dispatches media immediately. The image-loss defect in #79949 was correctly fixed by preserving attachments through the active-run steer path, but that still produces separate inbound turns and can produce multiple replies. It does not provide user-intent coalescing.

This causes:

- the model to answer before the complete image set arrives;
- multiple model runs and replies for one intended request;
- later text/captions to be evaluated without the full image set as one turn;
- unnecessary token and model cost.

#88264 described the same coalescing need, but was closed as a duplicate of #79949 before #79949 was resolved through its narrower steer alternative. The coalescing feature itself remains absent.

## Proposed behavior

```json5
{
  channels: {
    whatsapp: {
      batchInboundImages: true,
    },
  },
  messages: {
    inbound: {
      byChannel: {
        whatsapp: 2000,
      },
    },
  },
}
```

When enabled, image messages participate in the existing inbound debounce window. The existing key already scopes batches by account, conversation, and sender.

A combined turn should:

- preserve every image in receive order;
- combine captions and rapid text messages in receive order;
- produce one agent turn and normally one reply;
- preserve every original durable ingress lifecycle and read receipt;
- keep locations, replies/quotes, control commands, audio, video, and documents on their current immediate paths;
- keep current behavior unchanged when the option is unset or false.

## Why opt-in

Immediate media dispatch is a valid default. This proposal adds an operator-controlled conversation behavior for installations where rapid image sets are a common input pattern, without changing existing users or other channels.

## Acceptance criteria

1. With `batchInboundImages: true` and a nonzero WhatsApp inbound debounce, two rapid images from the same sender produce one inbound agent turn containing both images in order.
2. An image burst followed by text in the same window produces one turn with all images and ordered text.
3. Different senders or conversations never share a batch.
4. Quotes, locations, commands, and non-image media retain current behavior.
5. Every original message remains durably adopted and marked read after successful dispatch.
6. The default remains immediate per-message media delivery.

