## What Problem This Solves

Fixes #176244

`web_fetch` default Markdown output collapsed HTML table cells together and normalized indentation inside `<pre>` code. Code containing a longer backtick run could also end its fence early during text extraction, consuming the prose after it.

## Why This Change Was Made

The renderer separates table cells, keeps `<pre>` contents out of prose whitespace normalization, and chooses a Markdown fence longer than any backtick run. Inline anchors inside `<pre>` contribute their literal text and retain their whitespace, without introducing Markdown link syntax. `markdownToText` accepts a closing fence only when its run is at least as long as the opener.

Decoded NUL bytes in fetched prose are escaped before inserting a fixed preformatted-code sentinel. Restoration consumes escaped NUL pairs and valid sentinels in one forward pass, preserves malformed marker-like text, and avoids repeated whole-response searches. Code content bypasses the prose escaping. This keeps the renderer aligned with the HTML scanner’s bounded, forward-only handling of untrusted input.

Regression coverage combines a table and multiline `<pre>` code with embedded backticks, checks a linked URL with a line break and indentation in both Markdown rendering and basic text extraction, and checks entity-decoded prose that resembles malformed and valid placeholders plus a long NUL run. It verifies the full extracted text, including the paragraph after code.

## User Impact

Fetched tables keep readable cell boundaries, code retains its indentation and literal links, and text following a code block stays separate.

## Evidence

- Real call-chain proof: `node_modules/.bin/tsx proof-live.ts` called `web_fetch` through Gateway `POST /tools/invoke` in both Markdown and text modes against a loopback HTML fixture. It covers the table, multiline code with embedded backticks, a linked URL with internal whitespace, trailing prose, and a plain-paragraph control. Ephemeral auth, ports, and temporary paths are omitted.
